                <h2>This Week's Key Changes: Fund Flow Under Pressure</h2>
<p>This week, multiple overseas teams reported disrupted ad delivery schedules—not due to creative or targeting issues, but because of "leakage" in fund management. Three typical scenarios emerged:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Delayed fund arrival</strong>: Funds deducted via agents but not credited to platforms, with slow customer support responses causing lost delivery time</li>
<li><strong>Multi-account balance visibility failure</strong>: Managing 5+ Meta/TikTok/Google accounts simultaneously, operations staff unable to quickly identify which accounts need replenishment</li>
<li><strong>Monthly reconciliation discrepancies</strong>: Finance discovered differences between charged amounts and platform credits, with high追溯成本</li>
</ul>
<p>The root cause: <strong>lack of standardized operational metrics</strong> for fund management, with teams relying on manual monitoring rather than systematic alerts.</p>
<h2>Breaking Down High-Frequency Triggers</h2>
<h3>Trigger 1: Insufficient Transparency in Fund Flow</h3>
<p>Many teams prefer agent-based account setup for convenience, but this creates information opacity. When funds don't arrive, issues may stem from:</p>
<ol>
<li>Agent-side finance processing delays (weekend submissions)</li>
<li>Platform review cycle fluctuations (recent TikTok reviews have tightened)</li>
<li>Account holder information mismatch triggering risk controls</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Troubleshooting actions</strong>:<br />
- Save transaction receipts after each charge<br />
- Record three timestamps: charge submission, agent confirmation, platform arrival<br />
- Establish a charge timing log; follow up immediately if arrival exceeds 4 hours</p>
<h3>Trigger 2: Chaotic Balance Alert Threshold Settings</h3>
<p>Operations staff commonly wait until accounts empty before recharging—a fatal habit during peak delivery periods. A three-tier alert mechanism is recommended:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Alert Level</th>
<th>Trigger Condition</th>
<th>Recommended Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Yellow Alert</td>
<td>Balance &lt; Daily Spend × 1.5</td>
<td>Check delivery trends, prepare for recharge</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Orange Alert</td>
<td>Balance &lt; Daily Spend</td>
<td>Initiate recharge request immediately</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Red Alert</td>
<td>Balance &lt; Daily Spend × 0.5</td>
<td>Pause low-efficiency campaigns, ensure core delivery continues</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Trigger 3: Inconsistent Reconciliation Standards</h3>
<p>Finance-operations reconciliation discrepancies often stem from different calculation standards: platform reporting uses UTC time versus advertisers' natural day; invalid click refunds appear in the next billing cycle.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended practices</strong>:<br />
- Use platform "spend reports" generation time as the standard, not delivery-day data<br />
- Establish three-line reconciliation logic: recharge-spend-refund<br />
- Preserve original report screenshots during monthly reconciliation to avoid traceability loss when platform data updates</p>
<h2>This Week's Action Checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Audit fund flow channels</strong>: This week, map out all ad account recharge channels to identify information blind spots</li>
<li><strong>Set alert rules</strong>: Configure balance alerts for each account in internal management systems or spreadsheet tools</li>
<li><strong>Unify reconciliation templates</strong>: Finance and operations share the same reconciliation spreadsheet with clear calculation standards and time zones</li>
</ol>
<p>Fund management is the "infrastructure" of overseas delivery. Without stable infrastructure, creative optimization and ROI improvements are empty talk. This week, spend 30 minutes reviewing existing processes and strengthening these three weak points.</p>
<hr />
<p><em>Next week's watch will focus on: Common reasons for overseas ad account application rejections and material preparation guidelines.</em></p>
